# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-robogame-chassis.rules
#
# Stable alias for the chassis serial link.
#
# 2026-10-01: the chassis link stopped being the JDY-31 Bluetooth SPP tty
# (/dev/rfcomm0) and became the Pi's UART2, wired straight to the STM32's
# USART3 on PB10/PB11.  UART2 is enabled by `dtoverlay=uart2` in
# /boot/firmware/config.txt, which puts it on GPIO0/GPIO1 (physical pins 27/28)
# -- the HAT ID pins, free on this robot because nothing wears a HAT.
#
# ttyAMA2 is the name the kernel gave it.  That number is a property of the
# overlay, not of the wiring, so it is recorded HERE and only here: the runtime,
# the control hub and every script under pi-tools/ address the alias, and the
# number may be re-measured against the device tree at any time
# (`ls -l /sys/class/tty/ttyAMA*/device/of_node` -> .../serial@7e201400, which
# is the `serial2` alias, i.e. UART2).  The bring-up predicted ttyAMA1 and was
# wrong; the 2026-10-01 field measurement says ttyAMA2.
#
# This file is the source of truth for the INSTALLED rule, and the two had
# drifted: the Pi's /etc copy was hand-edited on 2026-10-01 while this one still
# matched rfcomm0, so re-running the pi-setup install step would have replaced a
# working alias with a rule that never fires -- no /dev/robogame-chassis, and
# the launch gate dies on "did not appear within 40s".  Keep them in step.
#
# udev OWNS this symlink.  The RFCOMM link maintainer service is gone, but the
# rule stands: that service's old `rm -f /dev/robogame-chassis` raced with udev,
# removed the alias under running programs, and was one half of the 2026-09-14
# outage.  Nothing else may create or delete this name.
KERNEL=="ttyAMA2", SUBSYSTEM=="tty", SYMLINK+="robogame-chassis", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"
